anv: HEVC and AV1 HuC BRC model a dyadic B pyramid per level, as media-driver

media-driver's VDEnc BRC models a B pyramid (HierarchicalFlag) per level: BRC init splits the GOP into P, B, B1, B2
frames and turns BRCPyramidEnable on (HEVC: media_softlet/agnostic/common/codec/hal/enc/hevc/packet/
encode_huc_brc_init_packet.cpp, GopRefDist != 3; AV1: features/encode_av1_brc.cpp SetDmemForInit), and each picture is
typed by its level (HierarchLevelPlus1 1/2/3/4 -> P_OR_LB/B/B1/B2: encode_huc_brc_update_packet.cpp, encode_av1_brc.cpp
SetDmemForUpdate, UPD_MaxBRCLevel 3/2/1 for GopRefDist 8/4/2). anv modelled every B frame alike (HEVC: GopB1/GopB2 0,
BRCPyramidEnable 0; AV1: only one B frame between the anchors).
Vulkan has no per-picture level; the app signals the dyadic pattern (VK_VIDEO_ENCODE_{H265,AV1}_RATE_CONTROL_REFERENCE_
PATTERN_DYADIC_BIT_KHR) and anv derives HierarchLevelPlus1 from the order distance between a picture's nearest past and
future references (GopRefDist at level 2, halved per level below), which is what a dyadic split gives (FFmpeg's
hw_base_encode_set_b_pictures). Flat GOPs are unchanged (byte-identical output).
B580, 1080p60 VBR 3/6/12 Mbit/s, HEVC with 7 B frames in 3 levels (Intel's default GopRefDist 8 pyramid at TU4):
BD-rate against the same pyramid without the per-level model -7.0% PSNR-Y / -5.1% VMAF on Netflix Aerial; against Intel
QSV defaults (hevc_qsv, GopRefDist 8 pyramid) +0.3% / -0.4%. AV1: the same model, active only with a dyadic pattern,
which FFmpeg's Vulkan AV1 encoder cannot produce yet (one hidden frame per temporal unit, vulkan_encode.c prev_buf_ref).

